| Result | What it excludes | What it leaves open | Role |
|---|---|---|---|
| Boolean-ring idempotent obstruction (LMB-002) | Deriving u or nontrivial phases by a direct Boolean-ring homomorphism | Scalar enrichment with additional choices | Interface boundary, routine algebra |
| Tensor/conditioning failures (MOD-001,002) | One naive closed all-nonzero or primitive-only shared process model | Restricted protocols, enlarged objects, changed readout, literal theory | Essential P01 limitation |
| Two binary settings (MOD-007) | Strong contextuality for the stated F2/residue-F2 contracts, any party number | Logical Hardy contextuality; three settings; other outcomes/residues | Candidate general setting-complexity theorem |
| Orthogonal-span and form obstructions (RES-009, ROT-008) | Particular proposed analyzer/correction and form-preserving repairs | Different gates, instruments, ancillas or scalar extensions | Supporting diagnostics |
| Singular teleportation transfer (RES-003) | Universal exact single-copy correction from singular resource under stated rank-one contract | Restricted input spaces, other tasks, literal multi-copy extraction | Core resource separator |
| Deutsch/DJ/BV (ALG-002--004) | Exact query savings under QXOR and complete linear readout | Different promises, access models, probabilities, postselection contracts | Core P02 results |
| Character/Fourier singularity (ALG-005,006) | The needed invertible character basis for this 2-group/char2 construction | Kickback itself; odd-order transforms; other algorithms | Mechanism separation |
| Simon/Grover (ALG-008,009) | Enumerated Simon contract and conventional Grover mechanism only | General all-size search/Simon lower bounds | Supplement, not headline theorem |
| Faithful probability obstruction (RES-013) | Positive probability on all possible events with ordinary normalization/no-signalling | Weak completion with further zero events | Known MQT background |
| Generic optimizer ties (CMP-002,006) | CM-exclusive final reduction on supplied admitted synthetic families | Lower recognition cost, reuse, provenance, natural-workload utility | Empirical boundary in B |

No-cloning and qualified no-deleting are useful checks but already have modal antecedents. Generic no-cloning should not be sold as newly caused by the CM ring. Strong contextuality does not require zero divisors: finite-field MQT already has it. The value of the chain-ring theorem is its valuation-sensitive classification and its explicitly restricted resource comparison.

Several no-go claims have positive companions: XOR interference despite no Born rule; actual kickback despite singular Fourier matrices; UNIQUE-SAT despite BV/Deutsch limits; useful folding despite generic equivalence; division-free updates despite process-closure problems. Those paired statements explain which ingredient is missing rather than merely reporting failure.
